      <h1 class="narrative-title">WA Mandatory Resale Safety Code Compliance</h1>
      <p class="narrative-lede">
        Navigating Washington State's mandatory statutory retrofits for real estate sales: water heater seismic double-strapping, carbon monoxide alarms, smoke detector compliance, and passing FHA/VA appraisals.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Real Estate Coach Callout -->
    <div class="coach-callout">
      <h3>💡 Real Estate Coach's Take</h3>
      <p>
        Here is the good news for both buyers and sellers: Washington State's statutory resale safety requirements are inexpensive ($20 to $30 per item) and take less than 30 minutes to complete. However, mortgage appraisers—especially for FHA and VA loans—will immediately flag missing water heater straps or missing carbon monoxide detectors as mandatory repair conditions, delaying closing. Sellers should knock these quick DIY items out <strong>before taking listing photos</strong> to avoid petty inspection repair demands or appraisal holdups. For buyers, missing straps or detectors are minor 15-minute handyman fixes that shouldn't derail a great offer.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Educational Body Section -->
    <section class="narrative-section">
      <h2>The Big Three Statutory Requirements for WA Real Estate Sales</h2>
      <p>
        Under Washington State law, every single-family home, townhome, or condominium transferred must satisfy three specific life-safety requirements before the buyer or new occupant takes possession:
      </p>

      <table class="comparison-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Statutory Safety Item</th>
            <th>Governing WA Law</th>
            <th>Required Location & Setup</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Water Heater Seismic Strapping</strong></td>
            <td>State Plumbing Code Sec. 507.2 / RCW 19.27.520</td>
            <td>Must be double-strapped (upper 1/3 and lower 1/3 of tank) anchored into wall studs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Carbon Monoxide (CO) Alarms</strong></td>
            <td>RCW 19.27.530</td>
            <td>Required outside of each sleeping area, in immediate bedroom vicinities, and on every story.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Smoke Detection Devices</strong></td>
            <td>RCW 43.44.110 (Greg "Gibby" Gibson Act)</td>
            <td>Required inside every bedroom, outside sleeping hallways, and on every story of the residence.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>1. Water Heater Seismic Double-Strapping</h2>
      <p>
        Because King and Snohomish counties sit in a seismic zone, unstrapped water heaters pose a major fire and flooding hazard during earthquakes. If a heavy, water-filled tank tips over, it can shear gas lines or rupture plumbing.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Double-Strap Rule:</strong> Code requires <strong>two heavy-gauge metal straps</strong>. One strap must wrap around the upper third of the tank, and the second strap must wrap around the lower third (at least 4 inches above the controls).</li>
        <li><strong>Stud Anchor Bolts:</strong> Straps must be secured directly to wall framing studs using 1/4-inch by 3-inch lag screws with washers. Plumber's tape (thin perforated metal strip) is strictly prohibited.</li>
        <li><strong>Under-$30 Hardware Kits:</strong> Pre-packaged, pre-approved seismic water heater strapping kits can be purchased at Home Depot, Lowe's, or local hardware stores for under $30.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>2. Carbon Monoxide (CO) Alarms</h2>
      <p>
        Carbon monoxide is an invisible, odorless poisonous gas produced by fuel-burning appliances, gas furnaces, water heaters, fireplaces, or attached garages. Under <strong>RCW 19.27.530</strong>, sellers must equip the residence with operational CO alarms before closing:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Placement:</strong> Installed in the hallway immediately outside each separate sleeping area and on every level of the home (including basements).</li>
        <li><strong>Types Allowed:</strong> Battery-powered, wall plug-in with battery backup, or hardwired units are all acceptable. Combination smoke/CO alarms are also fully compliant.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>3. Smoke Detectors</h2>
      <p>
        Under <strong>RCW 43.44.110</strong>, sellers are legally obligated to provide operational smoke detectors prior to transfer. Smoke alarms must be located inside every bedroom, in hallways leading to sleeping quarters, and on every level of the home. Sealed 10-year battery units are recommended for easy maintenance.
      </p>
